 "standard_code": "Grade 3: Addition and subtraction",
 "standard_url": "https://www.education.gov.za/Portals/0/CD/National%20Curriculum%20Statements%20and%20Vocational/CAPS%20MATHS%20%20ENGLISH%20GR%201-3%20FS.pdf",
 "standard_text": "Add to 999 and subtract from 999; use techniques such as building up and breaking down numbers, doubling and halving, number lines and rounding off in tens; check the solution with the inverse operation.",
 "meta_desc": "Free CAPS Grade 3 maths worksheets: adding and subtracting 3-digit numbers to 999, carrying, breaking up tens and hundreds, and checking. 100 questions with memos.",
 "key_big": "Add ones, tens, then hundreds.",
 "key_sub": "Check by adding back.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Break up the numbers: 356 + 238 = 300 + 200 + 50 + 30 + 6 + 8.</li><li>Add the ones, the tens and the hundreds; make a new ten or hundred when you need to.</li><li>To subtract, break up a ten or a hundred if the top digit is too small.</li><li>Check a subtraction by adding.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>356 + 238</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>500 + 80 + 14</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 594</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Forgetting to carry</td><td>14 ones = 1 ten and 4 ones.</td></tr><tr><td>Bigger minus smaller</td><td>In 52 − 27 you cannot take 7 from 2: break a ten.</td></tr><tr><td>Lining up</td><td>Ones under ones.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the Foundation Phase?",
   "a": "Grade R to Grade 3 in South African schools. Grade 3 is the last year; learners are about 8 to 9 years old."
  },
  {
   "q": "How big are the numbers in Grade 3?",
   "a": "Answers up to 999."
  },
  {
   "q": "Do Grade 3 learners use columns?",
   "a": "They use breaking up, number lines and building up; columns come in Grade 4."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I check 642 − 275 = 367?",
   "a": "367 + 275 = 642."
  }
